---
import SystemLayout from '../../layouts/SystemLayout.astro';
import ConstellationGlyph from '../../components/ConstellationGlyph.astro';
---

<SystemLayout title="apollo · abir deol">
  <a href="/passions" class="back">← passions</a>

  <div class="header">
    <svg class="lyre-glyph" viewBox="0 0 100 100" width="56" height="56" aria-hidden="true">
      <path d="M28 88V44C28 24 34 12 42 8" />
      <path d="M72 88V44C72 24 66 12 58 8" />
      <line x1="28" y1="88" x2="72" y2="88" />
      <line x1="36" y1="42" x2="36" y2="80" />
      <line x1="44" y1="38" x2="44" y2="80" />
      <line x1="50" y1="36" x2="50" y2="80" />
      <line x1="56" y1="38" x2="56" y2="80" />
      <line x1="64" y1="42" x2="64" y2="80" />
    </svg>
    <p class="eyebrow">why the name</p>
    <h1>apollo</h1>
    <hr class="accent-rule" />
  </div>

  <p class="lede">
    god of the sun, music, prophecy, healing, and the bow. the only Olympian
    said to be as comfortable on a battlefield as on a stage.
  </p>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <h2 class="sub-heading">the myth of the lyre</h2>
  <p>
    the story goes that Hermes, only hours old, hollowed out a tortoise shell,
    strung it with cattle gut, and built the first lyre. when Apollo came
    looking for the cattle Hermes had stolen from him, the infant god didn't
    apologize. he played. Apollo was so taken with the sound that he traded
    the entire herd for the instrument on the spot. he went on to master it
    completely, and the lyre became his emblem: the thing he's holding in
    almost every statue you'll find of him.
  </p>
  <p>
    what stuck with me about that story isn't the trade. it's that Apollo was
    already the god of archery and the sun before he ever touched the thing.
    and he still stopped to learn an instrument he had no reason to need.
  </p>

  <h2 class="sub-heading">why him</h2>
  <p>
    most gods get one lane. Ares gets war, Hephaestus gets the forge, Athena
    gets strategy. Apollo is the rare one who refused to specialize: archer
    and musician, healer and plague-bringer, the clearest-headed god on
    Olympus and the one who ran the oracle at Delphi, where the answers
    never came clean. discipline and instinct, precision and chaos, held in
    the same hand.
  </p>
  <p>
    that split is the whole reason he's the idol. two national titles inside
    a cage is one half of it, the part that's all repetition, weight, and
    control. the other half is the systems work: building things from
    scratch, precise enough that they don't fall over. neither side cancels
    the other out. Apollo's the patron saint of not having to pick.
  </p>

  <h2 class="sub-heading">the sun-mark</h2>
  <p>
    the little arc that crosses the sky on every page here is Apollo's
    chariot: the sun making its pass, the way it was said to every day. the
    lyre in the header is the other half of him: the one who put the bow
    down long enough to make something.
  </p>

  <style>
    .back {
      display: inline-block;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--muted);
      margin-bottom: 32px;
    }
    .back:hover { color: var(--accent); }
    .header {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: 10px;
      margin-bottom: 20px;
    }
    .lyre-glyph {
      overflow: visible;
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1;
      stroke-linecap: round;
      fill: none;
      filter: drop-shadow(0 0 10px var(--accent-soft));
    }
    h1 { font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.4rem); }
    .lede { max-width: 54ch; color: var(--fg); font-size: 1.04rem; }
    .rule { margin: 36px 0; }
    /* .sub-heading styling is shared, see theme.css */
    .sub-heading { margin: 34px 0 16px; }
    p { max-width: 62ch; line-height: 1.78; }
  </style>
</SystemLayout>
